---
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout
  title="How Vehicle Valuation Is Done in Kenya | Unitech Accident Assessor"
  description="Learn how vehicle valuation is done in Kenya, including inspection, documentation review, depreciation, and final reporting."
  pageTitle="How Vehicle Valuation Is Done in Kenya"
>
  <main class="page-main">
    <section class="content-section">
      <div class="content-grid">
        <article class="article-card">
          <p class="article-lead">
            Vehicle valuation in Kenya combines physical inspection, document verification, and
            market analysis to arrive at a fair and defensible value.
          </p>

          <h2>Who performs vehicle valuations?</h2>
          <p>
            Valuations are typically carried out by licensed motor vehicle valuers, certified
            assessors, and registered valuation firms. Their reports are commonly relied on by
            insurers, banks, legal teams, and public agencies.
          </p>

          <h2>When do you need a valuation?</h2>
          <ul class="content-list">
            <li>Before buying or selling a vehicle</li>
            <li>When arranging or renewing insurance</li>
            <li>After an accident for claim support</li>
            <li>For customs, financing, or court-related matters</li>
            <li>When reviewing fleet depreciation or asset values</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>The valuation process</h2>
          <div class="article-steps">
            <div>
              <h3>1. Physical inspection</h3>
              <p>
                The valuer checks the bodywork, tyres, interior, mechanical condition,
                roadworthiness items, and identification details such as chassis and engine
                numbers.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3>2. Documentation review</h3>
              <p>
                Logbook details, service history, accident records, import records, and any
                previous reports are reviewed to support an accurate conclusion.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3>3. Market comparison</h3>
              <p>
                Current asking and selling trends for similar vehicles in Kenya are compared,
                alongside model demand, age, mileage, and spare-parts considerations.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3>4. Depreciation analysis</h3>
              <p>
                Depreciation is considered based on age, condition, mileage, repairs, and use.
                Well-maintained vehicles with service records usually retain stronger value.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3>5. Final report</h3>
              <p>
                A complete report is prepared with vehicle details, photographs, assessed value,
                observations, and the valuer’s professional endorsement.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <aside class="sidebar-card">
          <p class="eyebrow">Typical fees</p>
          <h2>KES 3,000 to KES 10,000</h2>
          <p>
            Private car valuation fees in Kenya often fall in this range, while commercial,
            imported, or more detailed assessment work may cost more.
          </p>

          <p class="eyebrow">Need help?</p>
          <a class="button button--primary" href="https://wa.me/254755668653" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
            WhatsApp Unitech
          </a>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</MainLayout>
